  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Clathrus columnatus, commonly known as column stinkhorn, is a distinctive fungus characterized by its tall, hollow, cylindrical red fruiting body that emerges from a white egg-like structure called a universal veil. This species belongs to the Clathraceae family and is found in New England, where it fruits during warm summer months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Hollow cylindrical structure",
      "description": "The fruiting body is a distinctive tall, hollow column that is bright red to orange-red in color",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Emergence from universal veil",
      "description": "The mushroom develops from a white or pale egg-shaped structure called a universal veil or volva that splits open as the fruiting body emerges",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Foul odor",
      "description": "Produces a strong, putrid smell that attracts flies and other insects for spore dispersal",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Column stinkhorn typically grows in mulched areas, wood chips, and disturbed soils in gardens, parks, and woodland edges throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its unpleasant smell, the column stinkhorn's odor and appearance have evolved to attract flies and other carrion-feeding insects, which unknowingly help disperse the fungus's spores.",
